  "worked_solution": "We decompose the fraction into a sum of fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}+{e}^{-5s}}{s}=\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{8}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{u}_{5}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{5}(t)=u(t-5)$ and ${u}_{8}(t)=u(t-8)$ are the unit step functions.\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}} \\\\  & =2{u}_{8}(t)+{u}_{5}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-8s}+{e}^{-5s}}{s}$ is $f(t)=2{u}_{8}(t)+{u}_{5}(t).$",
